%% Answer plans for every past T7 question — RN volume only.

## Every past question as an answer plan

Use these plans in the last fortnight: read the question, say the plan aloud from memory, then check. Each plan is the skeleton of the full model answer in T7QA.

**2016 Q3 — Sites and forms of violence against women in Pakistan; how to eliminate it.** DEVAW 1993; argument: every site, three causes, combined strategy. Scale: PDHS 28%, 34%, 56% silent; SSDO 32,617 (2024); WHO 1 in 3. Sites: home (DV, marital rape, economic abuse; dowry violence, stove burning; honour killing — HRCP 405, Qandeel Baloch 2016); community (jirgas, vani/swara, Mukhtaran Mai 2002; rape 5,339 and abduction 24,439; Zainab 2018; acid — 2011 Act); workplace (harassment; domestic workers; 2010 Act, amended 2022); public spaces (s. 509) and online (PECA 2016); state (custody, police refusal, Zina Ordinance 1979 → 2006 Act). Grid figure. Why it persists: patriarchy, dependence (24.4%), impunity (0.5%). Elimination: prevention (schools, Rozan, scholars, media, BISP); protection (1043, shelters, VAWC Multan 2017, Punjab Act orders); prosecution (FIRs, women police, DNA, special courts 2021, non-compoundable); services and partnership. Five-P cycle figure. From impunity to accountability.

**2017 Q3 — Forms of GBV; workable strategies.** GBV defined; argument: use existing laws better. Theory: power and control (Duluth 1981), social learning, ecological model (Heise 1998). Forms by nature: physical (28%; acid; honour 405; stove burning); sexual (rape 5,339, 0.5%; harassment; child and forced marriage — 18 only in Sindh 2013, ICT 2025); psychological (threats, taunts, isolation; Punjab Act); economic (salary, dowry, inheritance; 2020 Act); custom-based (vani, swara, Qur'an marriage, watta satta — 2011 Act) and cyber (PECA). Tree figure. "Workable" = realistic, built on existing laws, measurable, acceptable; the gap is implementation (0.5%). Strategies: enforce laws (compulsory FIRs, women police, SSOIUs and special courts, DNA, witnesses, no pardons); services in every district (VAWC model, 1043, dignified shelters); prevention through schools, mosques, Rozan, media; economic independence (2020 law, BISP). Pakistan lacks enforcement, not laws.

**2017 Q8(a) — Short note: honour killing.** (~600 words.) Definition; men killed too; karo-kari, siyahkari, tor tora, kala kali. Scale: HRCP 405, SSDO 547, ≈0.5% convictions; hidden as suicide. Causes: honour, ghairat, control of marriage and property, jirgas, impunity; power and control; masculinity. Law timeline: Qisas and Diyat 1990/1997 (heirs forgive) → 2004 (defined) → October 2016 Act (life imprisonment even after forgiveness; Sughra Imam 2014; MCQ 2026). Cases: Samia Sarwar 1999 (Hina Jilani, Asma Jahangir); Saba Qaiser 2014 (A Girl in the River; Nawaz Sharif); Degari 2025; Qandeel Baloch 2016 (acquittal 2022). Assessment: re-labelling, hostile witnesses; remedies: non-compoundable, training, investigate every death, prosecute jirgas, protect couples, scholars, economic independence, shelters.

**2018 Q8(c) — Short note: government initiatives.** (~600 words.) Framework: Arts 9, 14, 25; CEDAW 1996, GR 19/35; SDG 5.2; GSP+ 2014; National Policy 2002. Laws: 2006, 2010 (+ s. 509; 2022), 2011 Acid and Anti-Women Practices, DV laws 2013–2025, 2016 honour, rape and PECA, 2021 Anti-Rape. Institutions: NCSW 2000/2012; women's police stations 1994; ombudspersons; PWPA 2017 (committees, officers); Ministry of Human Rights 2015. Grid figure. Services and courts: VAWC Multan 2017; 1043, 1099; Dar-ul-Aman; special courts and SSOIUs; GBV courts; BISP 2008; Zainab Alert Act 2020. Assessment: violence made public; but 0.5% convictions, FIR refusal, settlements, few centres, uneven provinces, "un-Islamic" attacks, 56% silent; make existing initiatives work.

**2019 Q7 — Structural and direct violence; Mukhtaran Mai.** Galtung 1969 (direct vs structural; potential), 1990 (cultural); iceberg triangle. Direct: DV (28%, 34%), rape (5,339, 0.5%), acid (2011 Act), honour (405), harassment, forced marriage, vani; 32,617 cases. Structural: literacy 53%, MMR 186, LFPR 24.4%, inheritance; Zina Ordinance and old pardons; FIR refusal, slow courts; jirgas (SC declared illegal). Cultural: honour, discipline, paraya dhan, misused religion. Pyramid figure. Case: Tatla clan, Meerwala, Muzaffargarh, South Punjab; Mastoi panchayat; brother Shakoor; 22 June 2002 gang rape; imam and journalists; ATC Aug 2002 (6 death), LHC 2005 (5 acquitted), SC 21 April 2011 (Abdul Khaliq life); ECL 2005. Outcome: schools, welfare organisation, In the Name of Honor (2006); 2006 Act; 2011 Act. Ending direct violence requires dismantling structures.

**2020 Q7 — Power-and-control relationship in GBV.** Definition; argument: best single explanation within an ecological frame. Instrumental violence; threat governs life. Dobash and Dobash 1979; Duluth 1981 (Pence, Paymar). Hub, spokes, rim. Cycle figure (Pakistani examples). Spokes: intimidation, emotional abuse, isolation, minimising and blaming; children, male privilege, economic abuse, coercion and threats. Walker 1979 cycle; Stark 2007 coercive control. Pakistan: honour, purdah as isolation, dowry and money, inheritance; Punjab Act recognises economic and psychological abuse and stalking. Strengths: selective, patterned, gendered; non-physical abuse; honour violence; Duluth practice. Limits: partners only; individual and structural factors (Heise 1998); Johnson 1995; mixed programmes; joint families and mothers-in-law. Policy: orders, independence, shelters, co-ordinated response, Rozan, scholars.

**2020 Q8 — Background of Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy's two Oscar-winning documentaries.** Karachi 1978; Smith, Stanford; Terror's Children 2002; Emmy 2010; SOC Films; first Pakistani Oscar. Saving Face: acid attacks (cheap acid, southern Punjab; revenge for refusals); no specific law, unregulated acid; Acid Survivors Foundation; Daniel Junge; Dr Mohammad Jawad; Zakia and Rukhsana; Oscar Feb 2012; Acid Act 2011 (336A/B; 14 years–life; Rs 1m); Hilal-i-Imtiaz, TIME 100. Compare figure. A Girl in the River: honour killings (405 in 2024); Qisas and Diyat loophole; stalled 2014 bill; Saba Qaiser, 19, Gujranwala, 2014, father and uncle, river, survived, forgave; Oscar Feb 2016; Nawaz Sharif "no honour in honour killing"; Qandeel Baloch; October 2016 Act. Themes: impunity, agency, film as advocacy, two faces of Pakistan. Criticism ("dirty linen") and response. Evaluation: media's power; enforcement must follow.

**2023 Q8 — Define GBV; forms in Pakistan; practical strategies.** DEVAW Art. 1; Punjab Act s. 2(r); WHO 1 in 3. Theory: Duluth, social learning, Heise. Forms: physical (28%, 34%; acid; honour 405/547); sexual (5,339; Zainab 2018 → 2020 Act; motorway 2020 → 2021 Act; harassment; child marriage); psychological and economic; custom-based (2011 Act), cyber (PECA; Qandeel, Sana Yousaf 2025), state. Grid figure. Practical = target causes (norms, dependence, impunity 0.5%). Justice (FIRs, women police, SSOIUs; DNA labs, special courts, witnesses; non-compoundable; prosecute jirgas). Services (VAWC model in every district, 1043, shelters, orders, Lady Health Workers). Prevention (schools, scholars, dramas, 16 Days, Rozan, 2020 law, BISP, age 18). Priority pyramid figure. National GBV database; enforce existing laws.

**2024 Q6 — What is GBV; forms under the Punjab Act 2016; consequences.** Definition (UN; DEVAW); scale (WHO; PDHS). Act: passed 24 Feb, gazetted 29 Feb 2016; "protection, relief and rehabilitation"; Art. 25; civil. s. 2(r) forms: domestic violence (lives/lived with, blood, marriage, adoption), sexual violence, psychological abuse (anorexia, suicide attempt, depression from oppression or confinement, panel-certified), economic abuse (food, clothing, shelter; taking income), stalking, cybercrime, abetment. Grid figure. Provisions: Family Court, 90 days; interim, protection (GPS bracelet), residence, monetary orders; helpline, DWPCs, WPOs, shelters, VAWC Multan 2017; penalties (1 year; repeat 2; false complaint 3 months). Consequences: physical and reproductive (51,100 femicides 2023; miscarriage, STIs, fistula); mental (2× depression, PTSD, suicide); economic (LFPR 24.4%, poverty); children (cycle), social (stigma; 56% silent), development (2% GDP; SDGs). Cycle figure. Assessment: progressive; CII opposition; mediation risks; slow implementation.

**2025 Q2 — Root causes of GBV; how they intersect in Pakistan.** Root vs trigger; ecological model; power and control. Patriarchy (discipline; honour — 405; paraya dhan; Saima Waheed 2004). Dependence (24.4%; inheritance; resource theory). Impunity (SSDO 0.5%, 1.3%, 0.1%; Punjab 225 → 2; Qandeel 2022). Customs (jirgas; vani; Mukhtaran Mai; Degari 2025; 2011 Act). Ideas (literacy 53%; social learning; misused religion; dramas). Crises (Malala 2012; floods 2022; COVID 2020). Cycle figure. Intersection of causes (norms × economy; economy × law; law × custom; ideas × institutions). Identities (Crenshaw 1989/1991: rural, Hindu and Christian girls, disabled, domestic workers, khawaja siras — 2018 Act). Ecological table and worked example. Break the cycle: impunity, independence, norms.

**2025 Q8 — Malala's achievements and activism, local and global — critical evaluation.** Argument: global symbol; local impact real but modest; state action needed. Background: 12 July 1997, Mingora; Ziauddin, Khushal School; TTP, Fazlullah, Dec 2008 ban, 100+ schools; 2009 operation. Early: BBC Urdu "Gul Makai" Jan 2009 (age 11); NYT documentary; National Youth Peace Prize 2011. Attack 9 Oct 2012 (Shazia Ramzan, Kainat Riaz; Birmingham). Global: UN 12 July 2013, Malala Day, "one child, one teacher…"; Malala Fund 2013 (Education Champions; countries); I Am Malala 2013; We Are Displaced 2019; Sakharov 2013; Nobel 10 Dec 2014 with Satyarthi, 17; Messenger of Peace 2017; Oxford 2020. Timeline figure. Global context: SDG 4; Afghanistan 2021, gender apartheid; refugees. Local: Shangla school; $14.6m in Pakistan; flood grants; Art. 25A advocacy; Islamabad Jan 2025, Islamabad Declaration; 25m+ out of school, literacy 53%. Criticism: Western agent, conspiracy, "I am not Malala" 2014, selective attention, distance. Evaluation and conclusion.

**2026 Q8(b) — Short note: masculinity studies and violence against women.** (~590 words.) Constructed, ranked masculinities; 1980s; Connell 1987/1995 — hegemonic, patriarchal dividend. Explanations: proof of manhood; entitlement (Kimmel's aggrieved entitlement); emotional restriction (Kaufman 1987 triad). Pyramid figure. Pakistan: ghairat, izzat; honour killings (405) as performance before men; acid attacks; Sana Yousaf June 2025; socialisation; dramas; marginalised men; jirgas police norms. Role: focus on men (against victim-blaming); learned and changeable; men's silence; solutions (Rozan 1998, White Ribbon, HeForShe 2014, Ziauddin Yousafzai). Limits: no excuses, funds for survivors, adapt concepts, mixed programmes. Complements protection and justice.
